{"answerable":true,"answer":"Yes, but only as far as needed. Under Article 12 you may depart from the driving, break and rest rules (Articles 6 to 9) to reach a suitable stopping place. Two conditions apply: road safety must not be put at risk, and the departure must be needed for the safety of people, the vehicle or its load. You must write the reason by hand on the record sheet, on a tachograph printout or in the duty roster, at the latest when you arrive at the stopping place.

The Commission's guidance note says this is only for unexpected problems you could not foresee. Examples are major accidents, extreme weather, diversions or a full parking area. It does not cover reasons known before the trip; your employer should plan for regular traffic jams. Enforcers will check your records to see that this is exceptional. You must not gain time from it, and it must not shorten your required breaks, daily rest or weekly rest.

The note dates from 2007, before the 2020 changes. The current Regulation also lets you exceed daily and weekly driving time in exceptional circumstances to get home or to the operational centre for weekly rest: by up to 1 hour, or by up to 2 hours if you took a 30-minute break just before and are going to a regular weekly rest. Any extension must be made up by equivalent rest taken en bloc, by the end of the third week after.","citations":[{"node_id":"561/2006:art12","quote":"Provided that road safety is not thereby jeopardised and to enable the vehicle to reach a suitable stopping place, the driver may depart from Articles 6 to 9 to the extent necessary to ensure the safety of persons, of the vehicle or its load."},{"node_id":"561/2006:art12","quote":"The driver shall indicate the reason for such departure manually on the record sheet of the recording equipment or on a printout from the recording equipment or in the duty roster, at the latest on arrival at the suitable stopping place."},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"This article does not authorize a driver to derogate from the Regulation for reasons known before the journey commenced."},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"foreseeing, for instance, regular traffic jams, weather conditions and access to adequate parking places"},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"major traffic accidents, extreme weather conditions, road diversions, no place at the parking area, etc."},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"a history of driver's driving records to establish the pattern of driver's performance and verify whether the driver normally complies with driving and rest time rules, and that the deviation is exceptional"},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"the driver should not have any 'time gains' by exceeding driving limit in search of a parking place"},{"node_id":"guidance-1:p1","quote":"the deviation from the driving time rules must not lead to a reduction of the required breaks, daily and weekly rest."},{"node_id":"561/2006:art12","quote":"by exceeding the daily and weekly driving time by up to one hour in order to reach the employer’s operational centre or the driver’s place of residence to take a weekly rest period."},{"node_id":"561/2006:art12","quote":"the driver may exceed the daily and weekly driving time by up to two hours, provided that an uninterrupted break of 30 minutes was taken immediately prior to the additional driving"},{"node_id":"561/2006:art12","quote":"Any period of extension shall be compensated by an equivalent period of rest taken en bloc with any rest period, by the end of the third week following the week in question."}]}
